  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nBroad surface anticyclone behind the cold front has been keeping \nconditions clear across the forecast area today, along with drier \nand cooler conditions. This will remain the trend through the \nweekend. An H5 high over the desert southwest will force any short-\nwave disturbances that emanate over the Canadian Prairie Provinces to \nremain north of the area. This will result in mid-level zonal flow \nacross the lower Missouri River Valley. Through the weekend, high \ntemperatures will reach into the lower and mid 70s. Early mornings \nwill be a bit cooler, with upper 40s to lower 50s possible. Light \nwinds and strong radiational cooling may help promote some fog \ndevelopment, but overall moisture may be to limited for it to become \nwidespread. \n\nA few short-wave trough axes will force the surface anticyclone \neastward into the Great Lakes Region for the start of the work week, \nbut most of the dCVA stays north of the area, and this is also where \nall the precipitation chances will remain, keeping our forecast area \ndry. Through the middle of the next work week, ridge over the \nsouthwestern CONUS will amplify and increase dAVA into the Central \nCONUS, allowing another stronger surface anticyclone to develop. \nThis will push a thermal ridge eastward, which will introduce a WAA \nregime through the middle of the next week that gradually take \nafternoon high temperatures back to the upper 70s and lower 80s, \nwith perhaps mid 80s for areas well south of U.S. Hwy. 50. Perhaps a \nshort-wave perturbation of some kind could generate light shower \nactivity, but current ensemble suites keep probabilities for \ndetectable precipitation at under 10 percent. Therefore, will keep \nthe forecast dry for the current 7-day period.",
